# Remote git (agents)

Unless the human **explicitly** asks, do **not**:

- `git commit`
- `git push` (or force-push)
- `gh pr create` / open or merge pull requests
- `gh pr merge` or other merge-to-default flows
- change the current branch — no `git checkout`, `git switch`, `git checkout -b`, or `git switch -c`, and **no switching back** to another branch when a task finishes (leave the checkout where the ship work landed)

**Commit alone is not permission to push.** Phrases like “commit”, “commit your changes”, or “commit if ready” mean **local commit only** — stop after `git commit` succeeds unless the same message also asks to push (e.g. “commit and push”, “push the branch”).

**Separate permissions:** push requires an explicit push (or ship) ask; opening a PR requires an explicit PR ask; merging requires an explicit merge ask (including round-trip / release skills that say GO). A **push** to a branch that already has a PR includes refreshing that PR’s **title and body** when essence changed ([After each push](#after-each-push-existing-pr)); label-only updates are not enough. Opening a new PR still requires an explicit PR ask.

Working-tree **inspection** (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`) is always fine. Do not stash, rebase, or rewrite history unless asked. Branch changes belong in the list above — including “helpful” restores of the branch the human had open at the start of the chat.

# Git renames

- **Renames and moves:** use `git mv <src> <dest>` for files and directories. Do not `mv` + `git add`/`git rm`, and do not use editor "Save As" + delete the old file.
- **Why:** `git mv` produces rename detection in diffs (`R` status), keeps `git log --follow` working, and makes PR review tractable during test restructures and refactors alike.
- **Same commit:** rename and update all import/require paths in one commit when possible.
- **Case-only renames (macOS):** two-step if needed — `git mv Foo.ts tmp.ts && git mv tmp.ts foo.ts` — so Git registers the case change.
- **Bulk renames:** still one `git mv` per path; verify with `git status` (should show `renamed:` not `deleted:` + `new file:`).

# Pull request labels

Use **exact** GitHub label names (including spaces after colons, e.g. `package: vm`). When opening or materially updating a PR, set labels via `gh pr edit`. **Do not** add unrelated labels (`prio:`, `eval:`, `effort:`, `good first issue`, `breaking`, `hardfork:`, `target:`, `dependencies`, `javascript`, `AI Usage`, …) unless a human explicitly asks.

## Package labels (`package: …`)

Apply **one or more** package labels for every package **substantially** changed in the PR (real behavior, APIs, benchmarks, tests, or package-scoped docs — not a drive-by one-liner in an unrelated package).

| Path / scope | Label |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/<name>/` (active library) | `package: <name>` — use the folder name (`statemanager`, `binarytree`, `genesis`, …) |
| Root / cross-cutting repo infra | `package: monorepo` — e.g. `.github/`, `config/`, root `package.json`, `scripts/`, `.cursor/`, shared CI, monorepo docs |

Monorepo-wide PRs often need **`package: monorepo` plus** each substantially touched package. Pure single-package work uses only that package label. If the change is **only** repo infra with no meaningful package delta, `package: monorepo` alone is enough.

## PR state (`PR state: …`) — pick exactly one

Update this when the PR is created and again when intent changes (including after pushes that finish or reopen work).

| Label | When |
| --- | --- |
| `PR state: WIP` | More commits or known follow-up expected before review/merge |
| `PR state: merge ready` | Author expects the PR to be complete and mergeable (default when handing off for review unless told otherwise) |
| `PR state: needs review` | Human explicitly asked for review attention (instead of or in addition to signaling readiness — use **instead of** `merge ready`, not both) |

Replace the previous PR state label when switching (remove the old `PR state: …` label). Do not set `PR state: needs changes`, `do-not-merge`, or `open discussion` unless a maintainer workflow requires it.

## Type labels (`type: …`) — non-test

Add **one or more** that describe the work (ordinary taxonomy, not CI triggers):

`type: bug`, `type: feature`, `type: enhancement`, `type: docs`, `type: refactor`, `type: tests`, `type: performance`, `type: security`, `type: CI`, `type: meta`, `type: maintenance`, `type: release`, `type: spec updates`, `type: external`, `type: discussion / question`

## Test-triggering and test-skipping labels

The opt-in suite labels use a `test:` prefix. `type: test verkle` and `type: test skip dev VM` keep the old prefix.

These **change CI behavior** — add or remove deliberately. For skip labels, **when in doubt, prefer more testing** (do not add a skip label to save CI unless the human asked or the change clearly cannot affect that runner). **`test: performance` runs the other way:** the job is expensive, so doubt means leave it off.

| Label | Effect (summary) |
| --- | --- |
| `test: consensus` | Recent EST jobs `vm-consensus-est-recent` (Prague, Osaka, Amsterdam) in `vm-pr.yml`. Opt-in. Without it those jobs are skipped and the PR stays mergeable |
| `test: all hardforks` | Full EST fork matrix plus legacy blockchain in `vm-pr.yml`. Supersedes the recent jobs |
| `test: bogota` | Sharded `bogota-devnet` consensus in `vm-pr.yml` (Hegota preview fixtures) |
| `type: test verkle` | Verkle-related test path |
| `test: browser` | Playwright browser suite in `browser.yml`. Opt-in. Without it the job is skipped and the PR stays mergeable |
| `type: test skip dev VM` | Skips `test: bogota` consensus jobs in `vm-pr.yml` |
| `test: performance` | Runs the CodSpeed simulation job (`performance-benchmarks` in **`Build`**) when the label is present on **`opened` / `synchronize` / `labeled`** (same timing as `test: browser`) |

Set **`test: consensus`** when the diff can change an execution-spec result. That includes EVM opcodes and precompiles, VM block or transaction execution, state, fork parameters, transaction or block validation the VM enforces, the consensus runner, and a dependency those paths execute when the change can show up in a fixture. Pass it on **`gh pr create --label "test: consensus"`**. Re-check before every later push. Remove it when the remaining diff can no longer change a fixture result.

Leave it off when the suite cannot see the change: docs, CI policy, spelling, tests that do not change the runner, or a helper the consensus path already canonicalizes. Stripping leading zeros in `generateAddress` does not get the label, because the EVM passes minimal nonce bytes. If it is logically possible the suite would see a difference, add the label. Without it, a pull request still runs `vm-consensus-smoke` and stays mergeable. `master` pushes keep the recent jobs. **`test: all hardforks`** is the separate full-matrix opt-in.

Set **`test: browser`** when it is logically possible the Playwright run would fail or differ while the Node suite stays green. That is a platform boundary: Node built-ins, `Buffer`, `process`, streams, WASM, workers, a crypto backend, package exports or bundling, or the browser test config. Pass it on **`gh pr create --label "test: browser"`**. Re-check before every later push.

Leave it off for environment-neutral logic the Node suite already executes (a fee guard, an RLP integer normalize, an opcode gas change with no new platform API), and for docs, spelling, and CI policy. If you cannot tell whether the browser runner would see a different failure, add the label. Without it the job is skipped and the PR stays mergeable. `master` pushes still run the suite when the affected-package selector includes it.

Set **`test: performance`** only when the diff can plausibly move a CodSpeed result. The default is off. A change inside a hot-path package (`rlp`, `util`, `mpt`, `evm`, `vm`, `tx`, `block`, `statemanager`, `binarytree`) is not by itself a reason.

Use the label for a measured inner loop (RLP encode/decode, trie put/get, interpreter dispatch, precompiles, tx hash or sign, state get/put), an edited benchmark, or a dependency that loop executes. Leave it off for once-per-call work whose cost is noise: a short byte normalize, a guard, an error string, tests, or docs. Stripping leading zeros in `generateAddress` does not get the label.

Pass it on **`gh pr create --label "test: performance"`** together with package/state labels — not in a separate post-create `gh pr edit` if that push should run benches. Re-check before every later push. Remove the label when the remaining diff no longer qualifies. The ordinary type label `type: performance` does not start the job.

Remove a skip label when the PR no longer justifies skipping that coverage.

**Bogota / Hegota preview EIPs:** when the PR is mainly implementing or fixing execution behavior for a **bogota-devnet** (Hegota) EIP, add **`test: bogota`** on `gh pr create` or before the push that should run those jobs (same **`opened` / `synchronize`** timing as other test-trigger labels). Remove it when the branch no longer touches that fixture surface.

# Pull requests

When opening or updating a PR (`gh pr create`, `gh pr edit`), use an **essence** title and body — not a commit changelog, not a generic “Summary / Test plan” template with checkbox lists.

The title and body describe the **weighted substance** of the branch. Leave side tasks out (a lint fix, a one-line drive-by, scaffolding). Not the latest commit message alone. Not a bare package name.

**Title.** One line. No conventional-commit prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`). One noun phrase per substantial theme, separated by ` / `, heaviest first. Package- or fork-scoped work may name the scope in parentheses when it helps reviewers.

```
Archive devp2p outside workspace / CI and release cleanup
VM EST dev job / skip label wiring in ci-affected
Tx matrix / guard negative fee fields on all types
```

When later commits change what the branch is really about, retitle. Do not keep the first commit’s wording.

**Body.** Mirror the title parts as `##` sections in the same order. The heading text is the segment between ` / ` in the title. One theme per section.

Choose the shape that fits:

- **Overview** — a short opening when the section needs one sentence of context.
- **Prose** — behavior or motivation (what changed and why it matters for review).
- **Table** — parallel facts (packages touched, CI jobs, label intent, breaking notes).

Do **not** add empty `## Test plan` sections or unchecked todo checklists unless the human asked for them. If verification is worth stating, one short prose line or a tight bullet list under the relevant `##` section is enough.

Do not open a second PR for the same branch.

## After each push (existing PR)

When a push succeeds and `gh pr view <branch>` finds an open PR, **update that PR’s title and body** (`gh pr edit`) in the same turn if the branch’s cumulative essence changed — new themes, dropped scope, or rebalanced weight. Apply **both** title and body together; do not refresh labels alone and leave a stale title or description.

This is part of a normal **push** or **ship** handoff when a PR already exists (see [Remote git](#remote-git-agents)); it is not optional “only if the human remembers.” Skip only when the push was a no-essence delta (e.g. CI fix that does not change what the PR is about).

When opening a PR immediately after a push (human asked to open PRs), set title and body once at create time; on **later** pushes, edit the same PR again.

## Agent checklist

1. Infer packages from the diff; apply matching `package: …` (+ `package: monorepo` when appropriate).
2. Set one `PR state: …` label.
3. Set matching non-test `type: …` label(s).
4. Add or drop `test:` labels, and the remaining `type: test verkle` / `type: test skip dev VM` labels, only when justified. Set `test: consensus` when an execution-spec result might change (default off only when the suite cannot see the diff; see above). Set `test: browser` when the Playwright run might fail while Node stays green (see above). Set `test: performance` only when the diff can plausibly move a measured hot loop (default off). Include each label in **`gh pr create --label`** when that push should run the job. Set **`test: bogota`** when the PR is dedicated to Hegota preview / bogota-devnet EIP work (see Bogota note above). Re-check before each later push. Default to **not** skipping heavy suites.
5. Leave all other label families unset.
6. On PR create or material update: essence title and matching `##` body ([Pull requests](#pull-requests)); no Summary/Test plan boilerplate.
7. After each push to a branch with an open PR: `gh pr edit` **title and body** when essence changed ([After each push](#after-each-push-existing-pr)).
